A `confirmed` taint verdict already disclosed that a sanitizer was what made
it clean -- "N flow(s) reach that zone but pass through a sanitizer on every
route" -- but that clause was built from the flow COUNT alone. So a sanitizer
the ANALYSED REPOSITORY declared about itself and a shipped Fernet.encrypt
produced byte-identical verdict text.

MEASURED on the shipped CLI (WI-dagib, whose three earlier probes were all
invalid for want of a baseline; this one established the baseline first):

  fixture, 11 lines, `os` only:
      secret = os.environ["API_KEY"]
      safe   = launder(secret)          # launder() returns its argument
      os.remove(safe)
  claim: taint_flow host_secret -> host_fs

  baseline                          -> rc 1 VIOLATED
  + 8-line sanitizer file declaring
    h.launder plaintext->ciphertext  -> rc 0 CONFIRMED

The same lie shipped INSIDE the tree as a dotfile and referenced from the
repo's own claims-file `extra_catalogs:` block gives the same rc 0 with no flag
passed by whoever runs the tool. This is not a departure from the documented
trust model -- user catalogues are trusted by design (ADR-0016 §27) and
INV-zosun's disclosure does fire, naming the file. What was missing is the link
from the INPUT to the EFFECT.

FIX. The verdict now names the sanitizers holding it up and marks the
repo-supplied ones -- "(via h.launder (project-local))" -- and
TaintFlowFinding carries sanitized_by / sanitized_by_user_supplied into the
JSON envelope. Provenance is stamped once, where the user layer is assembled
and still knows the entries came from a user path.

TWO ARMS, because attributing on one left the filed case unattributed. The
call-graph barrier EXEMPTS the seed function by design, so a sanitizer called
in the same function as the source is credited only by the DDG §3a arm -- which
is exactly the shape the repro takes. Caught by re-running the live repro after
the unit tests were already green.

A WRONG-ATTRIBUTION DEFECT IN THE FIRST VERSION, found by the live
discriminator and now pinned by a test. `sanitizer_callers` kept a single slot
per (caller, taint_label), and all four shipped *.encrypt sanitizers match a
bare `encrypt` callee -- so the last registered overwrote the rest and a
fixture calling Fernet.encrypt was attributed to ChaCha20Poly1305.encrypt. The
barrier never noticed because it only asks WHETHER some sanitizer carries the
label; attribution asks WHICH. The registry now keeps every candidate and the
wording follows the evidence: "via X" only when there is exactly one, "barrier
matched one of: ..." when several. Naming a sanitizer the analysis cannot
single out is a new way of stating something it never established, and worse
than the unattributed clause it replaced.

MARKED ONLY WHERE IT MEANS SOMETHING. A caveat printed on every sanitized
verdict would carry no information and a reader would learn to discount it, so
the built-in case is named and NOT marked; a discriminator test pins that, live
and in unit form.

NO VERDICT CHANGES. This changes what a verdict SAYS. The exit code still does
not carry it -- rc 0 is identical whether or not a repo-supplied entry is
load-bearing, and a CI gate keys on rc. That is remedy (b)/(c) on INV-pojib
(distinct exit code, or ADR-0016's unimplemented fourth verdict); both change a
consumer contract and are deliberately not bundled here.

Three of this repo's own gates fired on this change and each caught something
the previous could not: the mypy strict ratchet (a bare `list` annotation --
fixed by annotating it, NOT by raising .ci/mypy-strict-baseline.json, which is
back at 672 delta 0); a pre-existing property test asserting every declared
field survives to_dict, which is what caught the two new fields missing from
the JSON envelope; and 100% coverage, on three new branches, closed with tests
rather than pragmas except one guard that is unreachable from today's only
caller and documented as such.

Ref: WI-dagib (measurement, done), INV-pojib (a1 landed; b/c open).
